title: 'Dev launch round 2: production-home guard is spelling-proof, gnomond refuses dev homes, CLI is never first-party before M1.3'
summary: 'Review round 2 (reviewer blocking YAML fix; Aegis C1/C2). The production-home check now covers the realpath, the symlink target (dangling included) and darwin case-folding; a symlinked dev home is refused; gnomond and gnomon daemon start refuse any home holding the DEV-ONLY marker, so dev keys can only ever run in the gnomon CLI process. Spec §9.3: a self-declared CLI surface is first-party only after M1.3 helper attestation.'
symbols_touched:
  - '#dev-launch'
  - $daemon-lifecycle
  - '#identity'
tags:
  - decision
  - gnomon
  - dev-launch
  - security
  - review
body: 'Decisions: (1) the tripwire lives in daemon-cli.ts (gnomond foreground + detaching activation), not in startDaemon, because dev up itself calls startDaemon; (2) the device key is read once through the owner-only check and the same bytes are verified and handed out (no TOCTOU re-read); (3) a stop that rejects after the kernel exited is awaited and reported, exit 1; (4) an interrupted dev init is detected by a dev root key without the marker, and every unmarked-home refusal carries the remove-and-re-run hint. Runbook now states the M1.3 gap plainly: same-user processes can read the dev keys, so dev-log signatures prove nothing; ptys and browser history capture the 60 s launch URL; unset NODE_OPTIONS; Claude Code still writes ~/.claude. Pre-existing upstream drift found (not ours): compile71 binds G-LNK-11 to $move-1 but Appendix A does not list it.'
